{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790466516,"nonce":"fZwV7u1JeO3EuP8+PcV61ZP6fTM=","title":"Python: a chained comparison or unpacking runs the next library call after one raised","message":"`lo \u003c= x \u003c= hi` lowers to `le(lo, x) and le(x, hi)` with no pending check\nbetween, and `x, y = 3.5` reads every element before checking. When the\nfirst operation raises, the second still runs, and its own raise replaces\nthe first (zb_hook_raise entered with py$exc set). Seen in\nclass/typed_dunder_sites.py and tuple/unpack_primitive_locals.py under\nZYNTAX_TRACE_INTERP=1. Output matches today because both raises are the\nsame class, but an operand whose comparison or `__getitem__` is a user\nmethod runs user code with py$exc set, and a different second error would\nbe reported instead of the first.\n\nFix: a chained comparison tests `py$exc == null` before each later link\nwhen the earlier comparison is fallible; unpacking checks after each\nfallible element read.","files":null}]}